%S A399856 2,6,9,12,15,24,126,32,81,130,176,36,247,182,135,64,425,72,475,140,
%T A399856 189,286,207,120,625,1586,1107,868,3509,150,3751,512,1353,2074,875,
%U A399856 396,2331,988,897,160,902,882,1634,1364,405,2806,3807,240,5929,650,969,1612
%N A399856 a(n) is the least proper multiple of n whose factorial base expansion contains that of n as a substring.
%C A399856 The sequence is well defined as appending one or more 0's to the factorial base expansion of n in order to have at least A002034(n) - 1 trailing 0's yields a number with the requested properties.
%H A399856 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A399856/b399856.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..5040</a>
%H A399856 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A399856/a399856.txt">C program</a>
%H A399856 <a href="/index/Fa#facbase">Index entries for sequences related to factorial base representation</a>
%F A399856 a(n) = A399855(n) * n.
%F A399856 a(k!) = (k+1)! for any k > 0.
%F A399856 a(n) <= A153880^max(1, A002034(n)-1-A230403(n))(n) (where A153880^k denotes the k-th iterate of A153880).
%e A399856 The first terms, alongside their factorial base expansion, are:
%e A399856   n   a(n)  fact(n)  fact(a(n))
%e A399856   --  ----  -------  ----------
%e A399856    1     2        1          10
%e A399856    2     3       10         100
%e A399856    3     3       11         111
%e A399856    4     3       20         200
%e A399856    5     3       21         211
%e A399856    6     4      100        1000
%e A399856    7    18      101       10100
%e A399856    8     4      110        1110
%e A399856    9     9      111        3111
%e A399856   10    13      120       10120
%e A399856   11    16      121       12110
%e A399856   12     3      200        1200
%e A399856   13    19      201       20101
%e A399856   14    13      210       12210
